      {
        "id": "alg-poisson-nmf-multiplicative-updates",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "page": "ml02-latent-representations",
        "deps": [
          "def-poisson-nmf-model",
          "prop-poisson-nmf-loglikelihood"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "title": "Finite Poisson NMF multiplicative-update sweeps",
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-poisson-nmf-updates-preserve-positivity"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Trevor Hastie, Robert Tibshirani, and Jerome Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, second edition, corrected 12th printing",
              "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS",
              "locator": "§14.6, printed pp. 553–554, equation (14.74) and Exercise 14.23"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel D. Lee and H. Sebastian Seung, Algorithms for Non-negative Matrix Factorization, NIPS 2000",
              "url": "https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs6241/2019sp/readings/Lee-Seung-2001-NMF.pdf",
              "locator": "§4, PDF p. 3, Theorem 2 update formulas"
            }
          ]
        },
        "landmark": false,
        "aliases": [],
        "statement": "Input an admissible count matrix $X=(x_{ij})\\in\\mathbb N_0^{n\\times p}$ with no all-zero row or column, an inner dimension $r\\ge1$, a finite sweep count $T\\in\\mathbb N_0$, and strictly positive initial factors $W^{(0)}\\in(0,\\infty)^{n\\times r}$ and $H^{(0)}\\in(0,\\infty)^{r\\times p}$. For each $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$, perform one complete sweep in the stated order:\n\n1. Set $\\lambda_{ij}^{(t)}=\\sum_{k=1}^rW_{ik}^{(t)}H_{kj}^{(t)}$.\n2. For every $i,k$, set\n\n   $$W_{ik}^{(t+1)}=W_{ik}^{(t)}\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^p H_{kj}^{(t)}x_{ij}/\\lambda_{ij}^{(t)}}{\\sum_{j=1}^p H_{kj}^{(t)}}.$$\n\n3. Recompute $\\widetilde\\lambda_{ij}^{(t)}=\\sum_{k=1}^rW_{ik}^{(t+1)}H_{kj}^{(t)}$.\n4. For every $k,j$, set\n\n   $$H_{kj}^{(t+1)}=H_{kj}^{(t)}\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n W_{ik}^{(t+1)}x_{ij}/\\widetilde\\lambda_{ij}^{(t)}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n W_{ik}^{(t+1)}}.$$\n\nThe first factor block uses the current $H^{(t)}$; the second uses the new $W^{(t+1)}$ and the recomputed intermediate rates.\n\nReturn $W^{(T)},H^{(T)}$. The local well-definedness result proves that every denominator and rate in these finite sweeps is positive under the stated assumptions. This algorithm makes no likelihood-monotonicity, convergence, stationary-point, or global-optimality claim."
      },
